\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl1800\margr1800\margt1440\margb1440\f0\fs24\cf0
\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 They say Alchemy is the art of change. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Which, if you ask me, sounds like a fancy excuse to justify breaking the rules of the universe and cause an existential catastrophy. Everyone at my old school used to talk about it in such an awe, as if the founder of Alchemy himself was some kind of miracle worker. A prophet, even.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The story goes that long ago, in the Dunes of Alamiria, the most unlivable patch of sand you could imagine, where even cactus dare not to grow in there, people were forced to migrate into that piece of arid desert because of war. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Yet somehow there is one man who figured how to challenge nature itself, he put nature into a neck lock and beat the shit out of it.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He is a magician, tapping into the scraps of Arcane Energy drifting through the desert, and by shuffling around the power that had existed inside things, he harnessed them into a manipulable energy. He studied this arts for his entire life. Soon people called that magical art Alchemy.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 And legends said, he built the Citadel of Almir. Out of nothing. No wood, no sandstone, no metal, no water\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 just }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 poof}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 , and there Almir stood. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 I believe Almir\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s real, don\u8217\'92t get me wrong. I believe people can change the world with Alchemy. But a whole city appearing in one day is a lie. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 If that were true, this little town in the corner of the world called Weshaven wouldn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t be the dead-end of a town it is. Someone would\u8217\'92ve snapped their fingers and actually fixed something by now.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Change is possible. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 That is what I\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92m trying to prove today.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 I\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92ve had this one scrap of copied scroll for years now. I bought it off a drunk Alamirian merchant back when I was still in school. Not the wisest gold coins I ever spent, the dumbest impulsive buying I\u8217\'92ve ever done in my life.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The scrolls were supposed to be a recipe. Allegedly, the secret of turning lead into gold. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 There is only one problem, it was written in Old Alamirian. Which, as you might guess, is a dead language so dead even ghosts would need a translator. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 My classmates back then called me an idiot. They said chasing this scroll was like trying to catch a fly with a net. And maybe they\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92re right. Maybe this obsession has turned me into the Academy\u8217\'92s official laughingstock.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 But if I\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92m right this will be my magnum opus. The proof that change isn\u8217\'92t just some dusty myth in a history book. That Alchemy can still do what they all swore it could.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 And if I\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92m wrong\u8230\'85 then I\u8217\'92ll stop. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 This is the coin-flip of my life. Heads, I\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92m an Alchemist. Tails, I\u8217\'92m done.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 And today, I\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92m tossing that coin.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 My bedroom desk didn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t look like the kind of place miracles were born. Brass tubing snaked across the surface like drunken spider had tried its hand at plumbing. The glass beakers were secondhand, chipped at the lips, duct taped and glued. And the alembic\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 the pride of my apparatus\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 wasn\u8217\'92t even mine originally. I\u8217\'92d stole it from my school\u8217\'92s lab when I was younger.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 It was early. Too early. I opened the window wide, letting in air from the sea beyond. Waves rolled in the distance, grey and endless, but I only smelled metal. Sharp. Bitter. A stench I\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d fought with all night as the reaction worked itself down. I\u8217\'92d left the window open so I wouldn\u8217\'92t die in my sleep due to asphyxiation. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 And now\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 it was time.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 On the tray before me sat what used to be lead. Except it wasn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t dull grey anymore. Overnight, it had turned black, like tar. Exactly as the scroll described. One last step remained: purification. If the translation was right, if the recipe wasn\u8217\'92t some cruel joke, then the truth would shine out from the ashes.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 My hands shook as I adjusted the straps of my goggles, the lenses smudged but still clear enough. I pulled the breathing mask over my face\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 a ridiculous, bird-like thing that made me look like some plague doctor cosplayer. The leather smelled faintly of mold, but at least it\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d keep my lungs intact.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 I lifted the vial. The White Sulphur reflected the dawn light creeping through the window. Every instinct in my body screamed that this was either genius or suicide.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 And then, with a steady hand, I poured.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The black elixir writhed inside the beaker, bubbling like it had a personal grudge against the beaker. The fumes clawed at my mask, acrid and stinging, and the hiss of the reaction filled the attic like a living thing. For one sharp, terrible second, I thought the whole rig was going to blow apart in my desk.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The brass tubes rattled. Pressure built. My heart thumped in rhythm with every gurgle and spit of the mixture. This was it\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 this had to be it.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 And then\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8230\'85}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The froth settled. The bubbles died. The black faded away, leaving behind a dull, metallic sheen. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Not gold. Not brilliance. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Just\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8230\'85 lead. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Cleaner, shinier lead, but still lead.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 I pulled the mask off first, then my goggles, my hair damp with sweat under the straps. The sea breeze rushed in through the window, carrying away the last traces of sulfur stink. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 I leaned close, holding the beaker up to the morning light. No matter how I turned it, no matter how hard I squinted, it refused to become anything but what it was.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 I let out a long, bitter sigh.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Damn it,\u8221\'94 I muttered.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0  }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 It was tails.}
\par\plain \f1\fs24\b0\i1
\par\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\qc\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 -o-}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The counter at Welton\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s Apothecary had never looked shinier in its life. Not because I cared about its clarity, but because I needed something to do with my hands  to keep me from staring at that beaker of pure, useless lead rattling around in my head.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 I ran a cloth across the glass display, polishing it until my own sulky reflection frowned back at me. Then I restocked the shelves\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 painkillers, cough syrups, tinctures\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 making sure every label faced the exact same direction. Mrs. Welton would\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92ve been proud. Or mildly less annoyed than usual.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The front door jingled open. I turned.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Good morning!\u8221\'94 Markus filled the doorway like he always did\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 broad, round, awkwardly cheerful, as if politeness alone could make the world less cruel. He clutched a rolled-up newspaper under one arm. \u8220\'93You look angry. Did something happen?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93No\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 it\u8217\'92s just\u8230\'85\u8221\'94 I started, then sighed. What was I supposed to say? }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 Well, Mark, I tried to make gold this morning and all I got was disappointment and a nose full of sulfur.}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  Yeah, no. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You know what, it\u8217\'92s nothing to worry about.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Before he could press, I latched onto the lifeline tucked under his arm. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Anything new on the paper this morning?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Oh, it\u8217\'92s just\u8230\'85\u8221\'94 Markus unrolled the crinkled pages and squinted, fumbling like the words might rearrange themselves if he stared hard enough. \u8220\'93There\u8217\'92s a warning from the head council. Possible monster infestation.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 That woke me up a little. I plucked the paper from his hands and smoothed it across the counter.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The headline screamed in bold: }{\f2\fs24\b1\i0 BEWARE MONSTERS}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Below it, the article explained: }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 Professor of Arcane Studies at the University of Edenfield warns of spiking Arcane Energy readings from the Great Lake. Such spikes may destabilize the eastern coast of Argantheia, opening portals to the Arcane Realm through which Arcane Creatures can pass. Affected towns include Twin Pines, Black Shore, Ghorford, and Weshaven. Residents are urged to remain indoors until further notice.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You might need to be extra careful, Ed,\u8221\'94 Markus said, shifting on his feet. \u8220\'93They said it might be dangerous outside.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 I flipped the paper over and dropped it back onto the counter. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93That\u8217\'92s still a hypothesis, Mark. Did you see the word }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 \loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\af1\uc1\u8216\'91might\u8217\'92}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  in there? It\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s sensationalism. You shouldn\u8217\'92t get too worked up over this kind of stuff.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Well\u8230\'85\u8221\'94 He chewed his lip, like he wanted to argue but couldn\u8217\'92t find the words. \u8220\'93Alright.\u8221\'94 His eyes drifted past me, toward the back office. \u8220\'93Where\u8217\'92s Mrs. Welton? I have something to talk to her about.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Oh, she\u8217\'92s on the phone,\u8221\'94 I said, glancing over my shoulder at the muffled voice seeping under the door. \u8220\'93Seems important. I think she\u8217\'92ll be back soon.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The back door creaked open, and my mother stepped into the storefront. Mrs. Welton looked like she hadn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t slept in days\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 one hand rubbing her temple, the other clutching the frame as if the weight of the world had followed her out of that phone call.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Oh, Markus,\u8221\'94 she said when her eyes landed on him. Her voice was steady, polite, but I knew that look: concern buried under composure. \u8220\'93You came in early.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Morning, Mrs. Welton,\u8221\'94 Markus said, straightening as though he\u8217\'92d been caught doing something wrong. \u8220\'93I\u8230\'85 I wanted to talk with you for a moment.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Sorry, Markus. That will have to hold\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 I need a word with Edward.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Oh, it\u8217\'92s okay. I can wait.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 She gave him a faint, apologetic smile. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Ah\u8230\'85 I\u8217\'92m sorry, Markus, but it\u8217\'92s a family matter. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I\u8217\'92ll see you when I\u8217\'92m done.\u8221\'94 She continued.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Markus flushed, his round face turning the color of boiled crab. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Oh\u8230\'85 sorry. Excuse me.\u8221\'94 He shuffled off toward the brewing room, ducking his head like he was apologizing to the floorboards on his way out.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The moment the door swung closed behind him, the Apothecary seemed smaller, quieter. My mother turned to me, and the weight in her expression made my stomach knot.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 I watched Markus lumber past us, his ears still red, and disappear into the brewing room. The door swung shut with a soft thud. My brain immediately started racing through disasters like a gambler rifling cards. Did the stench from my experiment drift downstairs? Did I leave the front door unlocked again? Or\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 no, gods\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 did I forget to extinguish the flame in the brewing room?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 I turned back to my mother, trying to gauge how deep I was in it. She was studying me, head tilted, lips pressed together.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Why do you smell like metal?\u8221\'94 she asked at last.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 My mouth went dry. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I, um\u8230\'85\u8221\'94 I scratched at the back of my neck. \u8220\'93Did some experiments. In my room. Sorry.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Her sigh carried the weight of ten thousand disappointments. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I have told you not to do experiments in your room. We have a brewing room for a reason. And if you wish to work with stronger reagents, you can borrow your father\u8217\'92s workshop up in the tower. Do you understand?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Sorry, Mom.\u8221\'94 Then I tried to deflect with a crooked grin. \u8220\'93Is\u8230\'85 is that what you wanted to talk about? You didn\u8217\'92t have to send Markus away for that.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Her eyes softened, but only slightly. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93No, Edward. That is not what I want to talk about.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The pit in my stomach deepened.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93It\u8217\'92s about your sister.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Torrie?\u8221\'94 I asked, }
\par\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I just received a call from Liese Academy this morning,\u8221\'94 my mother said, her voice clipped and steady, though the lines around her mouth betrayed how hard she was holding it together. \u8220\'93Torrie has gotten into a fight.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {I let out a long sigh. Of course she had. Torrie was me all over again, only with more fire and less filter. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93How bad?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93She\u8217\'92s in the headmaster\u8217\'92s office,\u8221\'94 Mother replied. \u8220\'93Three other students are in the infirmary.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {I winced. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93That\u8217\'92s\u8230\'85 not good.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93No, it is not.\u8221\'94 Her eyes sharpened. \u8220\'93Edward, I want you to come with me to Liese Academy. I need to give a piece of my mind about this.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {My stomach sank like a stone. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Whoa, hang on. What about Markus? And Lydia? I can\u8217\'92t just leave them in the brewing room by themselves. They might need a hand!\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93We can afford some downtime,\u8221\'94 she said firmly. \u8220\'93There are hardly any orders this week.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93But, Mom\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 \u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Edward.\u8221\'94 Her tone had shifted. Final. No room to wiggle. \u8220\'93Go get changed, and prepare the car from the shed.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {I swallowed, my protests shriveling in my throat. Returning to Liese Academy was the last thing I wanted, but when my mother\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s voice took that tone, even the sea itself wouldn\u8217\'92t argue.}}